             "text": "In my humble opinion nobody, and I mean nobody, should have trusted the coldcard RNG. That's the whole point of being able to roll dice.\n\nThere are no 100% guarantees with any of these 'wallets' and i'm surprised that multi-vendor multi-sig isn't more popular. It could be a phone wallet combined with a \"hardware wallet\" (in a 2 of 2) even a bad one, and events like this would have been prevented  at least long enough for users to migrate funds. And even then nobody's phone was hacked or specifically targeted... Two separate systems (and modern phones are relatively secure) would have to both be compromised for the funds to leak and IMO the odds of 2 bad RNGs is way less than one.\n\nIf you think about it, you don't really need specialized equipment at all, maybe just to calculate a checksum from the dice rolls and the 'other' signer could be a freshly installed copy of Linux on a cheap latop. How many more people would have their savings if that was being taught rather than 'go out and buy a hardware wallet' which clearly failed. \n\nA little creativity and skepticism goes a long way and somehow we missed that.",
